{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["And the reason of <b>This is the law: when a man dies in a tent</b> — a man, when he dies while he is now in a tent; or Scripture speaks in the present tense, and the same rule applies if he dies outside and they bring him into a tent. <b>Everyone who comes into the tent and everything that is in the tent</b> — the vessels inside it and the tent itself are included in this category, as it says: \"And he shall sprinkle upon the tent and upon all the vessels and upon the persons who were there\" (Numbers 19:18). And Scripture mentioned the tent to indicate that it itself is impure for seven days and requires sprinkling. And further, because Israel were tent-dwellers in the wilderness, and Scripture spoke in the present tense, since the commandment applies immediately and for all generations. But the same rule applies to a house and to all coverings that bring impurity upon all the vessels and upon the persons who are there, except that a permanent structure does not receive impurity in itself at all. Now, Scripture mentioned regarding the dead person the impurity of contact and tent, but did not mention carrying, but our Sages (Sifrei) derive it by an a fortiori argument from the carcass, and extend it to seven-day impurity like contact. And so it is according to the plain sense of Scripture: it mentioned that with a dead human being and the slaughtered animal there is seven-day impurity and sprinkling upon contact, and it is already known that the contact of the Torah is literal touching, and touching by means of another object that carries it:"], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {0 "The Hebrew continues beyond this excerpt with a colon, indicating Ramban's argument continues; the translation ends at that punctuation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:06:56.260688949Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}